<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kraków</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cracow, Krakow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kraków is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the country's historic capital. Seat of a bishopric from 1000, it became the coronation and royal city, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> castle and cathedral, and in 1364 Casimir III founded its university. The court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> around 1596, but kings were still crowned and buried at Wawel. Its old town was among the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> in 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krakow.jpg" alt="Kraków" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrzej Otrębski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krakow_Rynek_Glowny_panorama_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hamburg, Bremen, Lübeck and Frankfurt were sovereign free cities in the 19th century and <b>Kraków</b> a free city from 1815 to 1846; Danzig was a free city under the League of Nations from 1920 to 1939 and Trieste a free territory from 1947 to 1954.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Two records survive from that time: the Kcynia record, with the first two stanzas and musical notation, and the <b>Kraków</b> record, with thirteen stanzas but no notes.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bogurodzica">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)</a></li><li><blockquote>…Mediæval Bæbes (1997), the boy choir Libera (2001), Tenebrae in an arrangement by Karl Jenkins (2004), Dominican friars in <b>Kraków</b> (2004), St Paul's Cathedral Choir under Andrew Carwood (2015) and the synth-pop group Erasure, whose electronic version of 2013 was the first single from their album Snow Globe.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gaudete">Gaudete</a></li><li><blockquote>It was first sung in public in <b>Kraków</b> on 15 July 1910, at a patriotic demonstration marking the 500th anniversary of the Polish-Lithuanian victory over the Teutonic Knights at Grunwald, and it quickly became popular across partitioned Poland.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rota">Rota</a></li><li><blockquote>During the first Mongol invasion of Poland, Mongol forces defeated the Polish armies of Sandomierz and <b>Kraków</b> near Chmielnik, between Kraków and Kielce.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-18">18 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Kraków</b>, Tadeusz Kościuszko proclaimed a general uprising against Russia and Prussia and took command of all Polish forces.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-24">24 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
